{"name":"Snake River","hex":"#45698c","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Snake River","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["a river located in the Pacific Northwest region of the United States"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Snake_River"}],"entries":[{"title":"Snake River","blocks":[{"text":"The Snake River is a major river in the interior Pacific Northwest region of the United States. About 1,080 miles (1,740 km) long, it is the largest tributary of the Columbia River, which is the largest North American river that empties into the Pacific Ocean. Beginning in Yellowstone National Park, western Wyoming, it flows across the arid Snake River Plain of southern Idaho, the rugged Hells Canyon on the borders of Idaho, Oregon and Washington, and finally the rolling Palouse Hills of southeast Washington. It joins the Columbia River just downstream from the Tri-Cities, Washington, in the southern Columbia Basin."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Adams_The_Tetons_and_the_Snake_River.jpg/330px-Adams_The_Tetons_and_the_Snake_River.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snake_River"},{"title":"Snake","blocks":[{"text":"Snakes are limbless reptiles of the squamate suborder Serpentes. Closely related to lizards and the tuatara, snakes are ectothermic, amniote, air-breathing vertebrates with an elongated rope-like body covered by overlapping scales, and they move around using undulatory, rectilinear, concertina, sidewinding or slide-pushing locomotion, with some arboreal species even capable of short-ranged gliding flight by flaring out the ribs to flatten and widen the body for aerodynamic lift. Snakes have skulls with significantly fewer sutures and more hypermobile articulations than their lizard relatives, enabling them to greatly widen the mouth and swallow prey much larger than their heads. To accommodate their narrow bodies, snakes' paired organs appear one in front of the other instead of side by side, and most only have one functional lung. Despite being tetrapods from a cladistic prospective, snakes lack limbs completely, although pythons and boids retain a vestigial pelvic girdle. Some lizards have also independently evolved a snake-like elongated body without limbs or with greatly reduced limbs at least twenty-five times via convergent evolution, leading to many lineages of legless lizards, who have eyelids, external ears and a fleshy non-forked tongue that snakes lack, although this rule is not universal."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Trimeresurus_sabahi_fucatus%2C_Banded_pit_viper_-_Takua_Pa_District%2C_Phang-nga_Province_%2846710893582%29.jpg/330px-Trimeresurus_sabahi_fucatus%2C_Banded_pit_viper_-_Takua_Pa_District%2C_Phang-nga_Province_%2846710893582%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snake"},{"title":"River","blocks":[{"text":"A river is a natural stream of fresh water that flows on land or inside caves towards another body of water at a lower elevation, such as an ocean, lake, or another river. A river may run dry before reaching the end of its course if it runs out of water, or only flow during certain seasons. Rivers are regulated by the water cycle, the processes by which water moves around the Earth. Water first enters rivers through precipitation, whether from rainfall, the runoff of water down a slope, the melting of glaciers or snow, or seepage from aquifers beneath the surface of the Earth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg/330px-Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Morley_Nelson_Snake_River_Birds_of_Prey_NCA_%289423934512%29.jpg/500px-Morley_Nelson_Snake_River_Birds_of_Prey_NCA_%289423934512%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Morley_Nelson_Snake_River_Birds_of_Prey_NCA_%289423934512%29.jpg/1280px-Morley_Nelson_Snake_River_Birds_of_Prey_NCA_%289423934512%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Morley_Nelson_Snake_River_Birds_of_Prey_NCA_%289423934512%29.jpg/500px-Morley_Nelson_Snake_River_Birds_of_Prey_NCA_%289423934512%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Snake River flows through canyons in the Morley Nelson Snake River Birds of Prey National Conservation Area, south of Boise.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMorley_Nelson_Snake_River_Birds_of_Prey_NCA_(9423934512).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Snake_River%2C_Hell%27s_Canyon_Road%2C_Idaho_-_DPLA_-_f093871da3a1279fc4ada15e8ecd4bcf.jpg/500px-Snake_River%2C_Hell%27s_Canyon_Road%2C_Idaho_-_DPLA_-_f093871da3a1279fc4ada15e8ecd4bcf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Snake_River%2C_Hell%27s_Canyon_Road%2C_Idaho_-_DPLA_-_f093871da3a1279fc4ada15e8ecd4bcf.jpg/1280px-Snake_River%2C_Hell%27s_Canyon_Road%2C_Idaho_-_DPLA_-_f093871da3a1279fc4ada15e8ecd4bcf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Snake_River%2C_Hell%27s_Canyon_Road%2C_Idaho_-_DPLA_-_f093871da3a1279fc4ada15e8ecd4bcf.jpg/500px-Snake_River%2C_Hell%27s_Canyon_Road%2C_Idaho_-_DPLA_-_f093871da3a1279fc4ada15e8ecd4bcf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&ut